      {
        "id": 215709,
        "name": "LeperKhan",
        "username": "LeperKhan",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/leperkhan/{size}/1894_2.png",
        "created_at": "2018-04-03T05:13:16.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Spiff\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/spiff/48/1865_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Spiff:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Not to derail my own topic, but I was poking around on the website of the company that made Gloomhaven and <a href=\"http://www.cephalofair.com/2013/03/dungeon-hunters.html\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">it turns out that the designer loves SotM</a>&nbsp;and started work on a game (not Gloomhaven) that tries to achieve the same goals of simplicity and variance of gameplay as SotM.&nbsp; As near as I can tell, the game was never actually produced.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>This post was from 5 years ago…did this game actually turn out TO BE Gloomhaven? You say no, but I assume that's just because he gave it a different name in this post and because he wanted it to be simple. Seems like he did make this game but realized that it was going to have to be bigger and more convuluted to achieve all that he wanted. Gloomhaven definitely has the hallmarks that he mentions in this post, most predominantly the different characters feeling so different from one another. Doesn't really matter in the long run; just a curiosity.</p>",
        "post_number": 30,
        "post_type": 1,
